('h2', "5.11  Termination of the Relationship"),
('b', [
    "The relationship normally ends when the **purpose is fulfilled** \u2014 the report has been issued and received.",
    "It may end by **mutual agreement**, by the **patient's own withdrawal**, on **referral** to another professional or institution, on **discharge**, or on **death**.",
    "A professional may withdraw **only** after giving the patient reasonable notice and reasonable opportunity to obtain the services of another professional, and after handing over adequate information \u2014 otherwise it is **abandonment**, which is both negligence and misconduct.",
    "Even after termination, the duties of **confidentiality** and of **record retention** continue \u2014 confidentiality survives the relationship and survives the patient's death.",
    "Grounds on which withdrawal may be legitimate: the patient's persistent non-cooperation, threats or violence, a request for something illegal or unethical, or a conflict of interest \u2014 but there must always be a **safe handover**, never an abrupt refusal in an emergency."
]),

('key', [
    "The relationship is **fiduciary** (trust-based) and **asymmetric** (unequal knowledge and power) \u2014 this is why the ethical burden falls on the professional.",
    "**Szasz & Hollender:** Activity\u2013Passivity (unconscious), Guidance\u2013Cooperation (acute illness), Mutual Participation (chronic illness).",
    "**Emanuel & Emanuel:** Paternalistic, Informative, Interpretive, **Deliberative (ideal)**.",
    "No single model fits every case \u2014 the model must match the patient's condition and capacity.",
    "India has a **Charter of Patients' Rights (NHRC / MoHFW, 2018)** \u2014 17 rights including information, records, emergency care, consent, confidentiality, second opinion, non-discrimination, transparency of rates, and redressal.",
    "The patient also has **duties**: accurate information, cooperation, respect for staff and other patients, payment, no demand for false reports.",
    "**Duty of non-abandonment:** once undertaken, care cannot be withdrawn without reasonable notice and a safe handover.",
    "**Professional boundaries** must be maintained \u2014 no sexual involvement, no gifts of value, no commissions, no money dealings, no treating one's own family for serious matters.",
    "Trust is built by **competence + honesty + respect + reliability + visible protection of privacy**.",
    "Regulation of paramedics in India: **National Commission for Allied and Healthcare Professions Act, 2021**."
]),

